@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "INSERT, UPDATE & DELETE";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT, UPDATE &amp; DELETE
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE are important SQL commands used
            to add, modify and remove records from database tables.
        </p>

        <p>
            These commands are part of
            <strong>Data Manipulation Language (DML)</strong>.
            They allow applications and developers to work with the
            actual data stored inside SQL Server tables.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- DML INTRODUCTION -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is DML?
        </h2>

        <p>
            DML stands for <strong>Data Manipulation Language</strong>.
            It is used to manipulate the records stored inside database
            tables.
        </p>

        <p>
            The three commonly used DML commands covered in this section
            are:
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>INSERT</strong> – Used to add new records.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>UPDATE</strong> – Used to modify existing records.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>DELETE</strong> – Used to remove records.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Sample Students Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            We will use the following Students table to understand
            INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE operations.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. INSERT Statement
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>INSERT</strong> statement is used to add new
            records into a database table.
        </p>

        <p>
            When a new student, employee, customer or product needs to
            be stored in a table, we can use the INSERT statement.
        </p>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            INSERT Syntax
        </h4>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO table_name
(
    column1,
    column2,
    column3
)
VALUES
(
    value1,
    value2,
    value3
);</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            INSERT Example
        </h4>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to add a new student to the Students table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(
    104,
    'Neha',
    22,
    'Nashik'
);</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            Output
        </h4>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT MULTIPLE RECORDS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Multiple Records
        </h3>

        <p>
            SQL Server also allows multiple records to be inserted using
            a single INSERT statement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(105, 'Kiran', 24, 'Pune'),
(106, 'Sneha', 22, 'Mumbai'),
(107, 'Aakash', 23, 'Nashik');</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. UPDATE Statement
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>UPDATE</strong> statement is used to modify
            existing records in a table.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, if a student's city or age changes, we can
            update the existing record instead of inserting a new one.
        </p>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            UPDATE Syntax
        </h4>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE table_name
SET column_name = value
WHERE condition;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            UPDATE Example
        </h4>

        <p>
            Suppose Rahul's age changes from 22 to 23.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 23
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            Output
        </h4>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE MULTIPLE COLUMNS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Update Multiple Columns
        </h3>

        <p>
            Multiple columns can be modified using the same UPDATE
            statement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 24,
    City = 'Mumbai'
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query changes both the Age and City of the student
            whose StudentId is 101.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE WITHOUT WHERE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UPDATE Without WHERE
        </h3>

        <p>
            If the WHERE clause is not specified, the UPDATE statement
            can modify the selected column for every row in the table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Important:</strong>
            Always check the WHERE condition before executing an UPDATE
            when only specific records should be changed.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DELETE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. DELETE Statement
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DELETE</strong> statement is used to remove
            records from a database table.
        </p>

        <p>
            We can delete a specific record by using a WHERE condition.
        </p>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            DELETE Syntax
        </h4>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DELETE FROM table_name
WHERE condition;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            DELETE Example
        </h4>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to remove the student whose StudentId is
            104.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentId = 104;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            Output
        </h4>

        <p>
            After executing the query, the record with StudentId 104
            will no longer exist in the table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DELETE WITHOUT WHERE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DELETE Without WHERE
        </h3>

        <p>
            If DELETE is executed without a WHERE condition, all rows
            from the table are targeted for deletion.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DELETE FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Warning:</strong>
            This statement deletes all rows from the Students table.
            Use it only when deleting all records is actually intended.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT UPDATE DELETE COMPARISON -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT vs UPDATE vs DELETE
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Command</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Operation</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>INSERT</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Adds new records.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Creates rows.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Add new student
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>UPDATE</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Modifies existing records.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Changes rows.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Change student age
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>DELETE</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Removes records.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Deletes rows.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Remove student
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMPLETE FLOW -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT, UPDATE &amp; DELETE Flow
        </h2>

        <p>
            These three commands perform different operations on table
            data.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                    Table
                      |
          ------------------------
          |          |           |
        INSERT     UPDATE      DELETE
          |          |           |
         Add       Modify      Remove
          |          |           |
          ------------------------
                      |
                 Table Data
            </code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- REAL WORLD EXAMPLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example 🌍🤓
        </h2>

        <p>
            Consider a Student Management System.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Situation</th>
                        <th>SQL Command</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>New student joins the institute</td>
                        <td><strong>INSERT</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Student changes city</td>
                        <td><strong>UPDATE</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Student record needs to be removed</td>
                        <td><strong>DELETE</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    INSERT is used to add new records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    UPDATE is used to modify existing records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DELETE is used to remove records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use WHERE carefully with UPDATE.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use WHERE carefully with DELETE.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how INSERT is used to add
                records, UPDATE is used to modify records and DELETE is
                used to remove records. We also learned their syntax,
                practical examples, output and important precautions
                when using WHERE conditions.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


